-- Retired: chess_leaderboard(integer) and chess_opponents(bytea, integer).
--
-- Both answered "what is this player's record" by AGGREGATING evidence at query time:
-- chess_leaderboard grouped every colour-header row in the corpus (~400k rows for ~200k
-- games, ~10s) and chess_opponents regrouped one player's whole career by opponent. They
-- were correct, and they were the wrong shape. A record that takes ten seconds has to be
-- cached to be usable, and the cache then has to be kept fresh -- state standing in for a
-- fold the substrate exists to have already done.
--
-- Each game now carries its result onto the player in the aggregating lane at ingest, so
-- the same two questions are reads of consensus cells: chess.ranked() orders by consensus.eff_mu
-- over one relation partition, chess.head_to_head() reads one folded cell per pairing.
-- witness_count IS games played. Nothing is recomputed and nothing is cached.
--
-- Dropped rather than left in place: two implementations of one fact is the duplication
-- the engineering rules forbid without a documented reason, and the surviving pair is
-- strictly better (Glicko-2 weighs WHO you beat; a win ratio cannot). The install SQL is
-- CREATE-OR-REPLACE and never drops, so on the UPGRADE path these catalog rows would
-- linger while a fresh CREATE EXTENSION would not have them; this DROP makes the two match.
--
-- chess.player_record() is NOT retired. The fold yields strength, not the W/D/L split, and
-- that split is a genuinely different question -- answered by index lookups on ONE player
-- (74ms measured over a 1341-game career), which is a read, not an aggregate.
DROP FUNCTION IF EXISTS chess_leaderboard(integer);
DROP FUNCTION IF EXISTS chess_opponents(bytea, integer);
